 ## Background
-The concept of multiresolution data representation using octrees for hierarchical volume "bricking" was introduced in early volume visualization research [S1]. Kähler and Hege extended this by rendering AMR data using 3DTM, partitioning the volume into axis-aligned bricks of uniform resolution and extending the tree-based structure for large, sparse datasets [S1]. The Berger-Colella AMR data structure, which underpins many modern block-structured AMR implementations, organizes data into bricks containing cell-centered values [S2, unofficial]. Recent developments focus on continuous reconstruction strategies, such as the AMR Basis Reconstruction Filter, which addresses stitching artifacts at level boundaries by blending contributions from all levels simultaneously, avoiding the need for unstructured mesh subdivision [S2, unofficial].
+Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) was originally developed for computational fluid dynamics to handle multiscale phenomena efficiently. The Berger-Colella AMR framework is a standard implementation for structured grid refinement [S2, unofficial]. In visualization, AMR data structures have been adapted from their computational origins to support efficient rendering of large, sparse datasets. Early approaches included octree-based multiresolution encoding [S1]. Later, techniques like 3DTM (3D Texture Mapping) were extended to handle AMR trees by partitioning the volume into axis-aligned bricks of uniform resolution per level [S1]. The challenge of rendering cell-centered AMR data without artifacts at level boundaries led to the development of various reconstruction strategies, including subdivision into unstructured meshes [S2, unofficial] and basis function blending [S2, unofficial].
 
